{"cuisine/inca-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/inca-cuisine","n":"Inca cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Peru","intro":[["Today, ",["Quechua","cuisine/quechua-foodways"]," and ",["Aymara","cuisine/aymara-foodways"]," farmers in the Andes still grow hundreds of native potatoes and make ",["Chuño","ingredient/chuno"],"."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cultural Survival, Quechua guardians of the potato","url":"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/fr/publications/cultural-survival-quarterly/quechua-guardians-potato"},{"name":"Cultural Survival, Chuño, a symbol of food sovereignty","url":"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/news/chuno-symbol-food-sovereignty-and-ancestral-food-preservation"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"inca-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inca_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/57/PachaComer.jpg/500px-PachaComer.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Xauxa","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PachaComer.jpg"}},"cuisine/kiribati-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kiribati-cuisine","n":"Kiribati cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Kiribati","intro":[["Kiribati, officially the Republic of Kiribati, is an archipelagic country spanning the Micronesia and Polynesia sub-regions of Oceania in the central Pacific Ocean. The state comprises 32 atolls and other islands and one remote raised coral island, Banaba. Its total land area is 811 km2 (313 sq mi) dispersed over 3,441,810 km2 (1,328,890 sq mi) of ocean. The spread of the country's islands, from Banaba in the west to Kiritimati in the east, straddles the equator and the 180th meridian. The International Date Line goes around Kiribati and swings far to the east, almost reaching 150°W. This brings Kiribati's easternmost islands, the southern Line Islands south of Hawaii, into the same day as the Gilbert Islands and places them in the most advanced time zone on Earth: UTC+14."],["The permanent population of Kiribati is just over 138,000 as of the 2026 census, and more than half live on Tarawa. There is also a significant number of I‑Kiribati forming a diaspora, the largest of which still growing via emigration is probably in New Zealand; historically, diasporic communities were created through over-population resettlement in the Solomon Islands on Ghizo and Wagina, and through intermarriage in the United Kingdom and United States. Another country with growing diasporic communities born of recent labour immigration is Australia. The I‑Kiribati are a Micronesian people who speak the Gilbertese language."],["The Gilberts were politically autonomous of each other, more or less, and anywhere else, until annexed as a protectorate in 1892 by the British; they had no political connection then with the Phoenix or Line islands. This annexation was ended in 1979, when Kiribati gained its independence from the United Kingdom, becoming a sovereign state in 1979. The sovereign state included the Gilberts, the Phoenix and Line islands and Banaba. The capital, Tarawa, now the most populated area, consists of a number of islets, connected by a series of causeways. These comprise about half the area of Tarawa Atoll. Prior to its independence the country exported phosphate, but the mine, on Banaba, is virtually exhausted and no longer viable. The descendants of the Banabans mostly live in exile on Rabi in Fiji but are represented in the Kiribati Parliament."],["Fisheries, subsistence agriculture and the export of copra drive much of the economy, particularly on outer islands away from Tarawa. Kiribati is one of the least developed countries in the world and its government is highly dependent on fishing licence fees from foreign fleets and international capital aid to finance infrastructure and other projects. Many families rely on remittances from circular labour migrants (seafarers, agricultural workers, urban service workers). They also work for the government or for private business and non-business organisations, or run trade stores and small private cooperative-like businesses. The government now pays benefits to the unemployed on Tarawa and to the elderly."],["Kiribati is a member of the Pacific Community, Commonwealth of Nations, the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank, and the Organisation of African, Caribbean and Pacific States, and became a full member of the United Nations in 1999."],["As an atoll (and other low-lying island) nation, Kiribati is acutely vulnerable to sea-level rise and climate change. This is in addition to threats from tsunamis since time-immemorial. Addressing climate change has been a central part of its international policy, as a member of the Alliance of Small Island States. In Kiribati, climate change and extreme weather events are predicted to become more frequent. Additionally, existing socio-economic and environmental pressures are intensifying."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kiribati-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiribati#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Boki ni Kuuka: Recipes from Kiribati (Live & Learn)","ref":"site/livelearn-org","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"livelearn.org","note":"Free PDF cookbook of community recipes from Tarawa, Abaiang and Kiritimati, produced by the NGO Live & Learn.","url":"https://livelearn.org/resources/boki-ni-kuuka-recipes-from-kiribati/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"cuisine/nahua-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nahua-foodways","n":"Nahua foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Central and eastern Mexico, among other places the Huasteca and the Sierra de Zongolica, Veracruz","names":"Nahua","intro":[["Nahua communities speak Nahuatl, and many still farm the mīllah, a cornfield where much more than corn grows. In Chicontepec, in the Huasteca of Veracruz, fresh tlaxcalli, tortillas, are made at midday, and etl, ",["beans","ingredient/beans"],", go into refried beans, soups and bean ",["tamales","dish/tamale"]," called piquiz, nearly always with ",["chile","ingredient/chili-pepper"],"."],["In the mountains of Zongolica, Veracruz, the Siwalcentli Collective of Nahua women shares their recipes: tesmole, a stew served with little balls of ",["masa","ingredient/masa"],", fresh corn tamales, tortillas cooked on a clay comal, wild greens like mixquilitl, and popotocas, insects eaten in soup or fried with salsa. Epazote, corn, squash and tiny citlaltomatl tomatoes all grow close to home."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Secretaría de Cultura and Colectivo Siwalcentli on Google Arts & Culture, Nahua Siwameh itech Tlekuille","url":"https://artsandculture.google.com/story/JgWhWhzqlzrcMQ"},{"name":"University of Texas at Austin, Tlahtolli, Our cornfield and our food","url":"https://tlahtolli.coerll.utexas.edu/tlamachtiliztli-20-lesson-20-nomillah-huah-notlacualiztli-our-cornfield-and-our-food/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nahua-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Nahua Siwameh itech Tlekuille","ref":"site/artsandculture-google-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"artsandculture.google.com","note":"Recipes and photos from Nahua women cooks of the Sierra de Zongolica, Veracruz.","url":"https://artsandculture.google.com/story/JgWhWhzqlzrcMQ","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Corn husks","ingredient/corn-husks"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Masa","ingredient/masa"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Aztec cuisine","cuisine/aztec-cuisine"],["Nixtamalization","ingredient/nixtamalization"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a5/Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg/500px-Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg","w":500,"h":382,"by":"Nsaum75","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg","of":"Tamale"}},"dish/alinea-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/alinea-restaurant","n":"Alinea (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Molecular gastronomy","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["Alinea is a restaurant in Chicago, Illinois, United States. It is owned by chef Grant Achatz and Nick Kokonas, and known for its \"maximalist modernist cooking\". From 2011 to 2024, Alinea was awarded three stars by the Michelin Guide; it was downgraded to two Michelin stars in 2025."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-alinea-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alinea_(restaurant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Molecular gastronomy","cuisine/molecular-gastronomy"]]},{"h":"More from Molecular gastronomy","links":[["Biko (restaurant)","dish/biko-restaurant"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/39/Alinea_-_Front.jpg/500px-Alinea_-_Front.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rebecca Siegel","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alinea_-_Front.jpg"}},"ingredient/tahini":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tahini","n":"Tahini","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Tahini is a Middle Eastern condiment (a seed butter) made from ground sesame seeds. The most common variety comes from hulled seeds, but unhulled ones can also be used; the latter variety is slightly bitter, but more nutritious. The seeds are more commonly roasted than raw. Tahini can be served by itself (as a dip), made into a salad dressing, or used as a major ingredient in hummus, baba ghanoush, or halva."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tahini"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahini"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine",[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Hummus","dish/hummus"],["Sabich","dish/sabich"]]],["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"]]],["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine",[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"]]],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine",[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"]]],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine",[["Hummus","dish/hummus"]]],["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",[["Hummus","dish/hummus"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Amba","ingredient/amba"],["Amba (condiment)","ingredient/amba-condiment"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hummus","ingredient/hummus"],["Israeli salad","ingredient/israeli-salad"],["Laffa","ingredient/laffa"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Salad","ingredient/salad"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/39/Tahina.JPG/500px-Tahina.JPG","w":500,"h":376,"by":"Gilabrand","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahina.JPG"}},"ingredient/nigella-sativa":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/nigella-sativa","n":"Nigella sativa","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Nigella sativa (common names, black caraway, black cumin, nigella, charnushka, or kalonji) is an annual flowering plant in the family Ranunculaceae, native to western Asia (the Levant, Cyprus, Turkey, Iran and Iraq), and eastern Europe (Bulgaria and Romania). It is used as a spice in various food preparations."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-nigella-sativa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nigella_sativa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Betawi cuisine","cuisine/betawi-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Biancaea sappan","ingredient/biancaea-sappan"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Illicium verum","ingredient/illicium-verum"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Piper retrofractum","ingredient/piper-retrofractum"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/53/Nsativa001Wien.jpg/500px-Nsativa001Wien.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"AndreHolz at English Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nsativa001Wien.jpg"}},"dish/bread-sauce":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bread-sauce","n":"Bread sauce","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A bread sauce is a British warm or cold sauce made with milk, which is thickened with bread crumbs, typically eaten with roast chicken or turkey."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bread-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","ref":"site/greatbritishchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["68–86 Bar and Restaurant","dish/68-86-bar-and-restaurant"],["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dinner by Heston Blumenthal","dish/dinner-by-heston-blumenthal"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Gidleigh Park","dish/gidleigh-park"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Restaurant Andrew Fairlie","dish/restaurant-andrew-fairlie"],["Restaurant James Sommerin","dish/restaurant-james-sommerin"],["Rhodes Twenty Four","dish/rhodes-twenty-four"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Rose & Crown Pub & Dining Room","dish/rose-crown-pub-dining-room"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d9/Bread_sauce.jpg/500px-Bread_sauce.jpg","w":500,"h":444,"by":"I, ElinorD","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bread_sauce.jpg"}},"dish/chimaek":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chimaek","n":"Chimaek","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from South Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chimaek is a pairing of fried chicken (either plain huraideu or spicy yangnyeom) and beer, served as anju (English: food with alcohol) in the evening in many South Korean restaurants, including a number of specialized chains."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chimaek"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimaek"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Korean fried chicken","ingredient/korean-fried-chicken"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from South Korean cuisine","links":[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dak-galbi","dish/dak-galbi"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jinju bibimbap","dish/jinju-bibimbap"],["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/46/Iksan_City_48_Korean_Style_Fried_chicken.jpg/500px-Iksan_City_48_Korean_Style_Fried_chicken.jpg","w":500,"h":269,"by":"KOREA.NET","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iksan_City_48_Korean_Style_Fried_chicken.jpg"}},"dish/es-doger":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/es-doger","n":"Es doger","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Es doger is an Indonesian coconut milk-based shaved ice beverage with pinkish color often served as a dessert. It is a specialty of Bandung, West Java. The main, or base, part is sugared sweet coconut milk-based ice in pink syrup, served with pacar cina merah delima (red tapioca pearls), avocado, cassava tapai, ketan hitam (black glutinous rice) tapai, jackfruit, diced bread and condensed milk. The condensed milk can be plain (white) or chocolate-flavoured. Es doger gains its pinkish color from rozen (rose) syrup, cocopandan syrup, or pink food coloring. Es doger is commonly sold by travelling vendor carts in major Indonesian cities, mainly in Bandung, Jakarta, Malang and Surabaya."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-es-doger"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_doger"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Avocado","ingredient/avocado"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Ice","ingredient/ice"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Platostoma palustre","ingredient/platostoma-palustre"],["Sugar","ing